1789:**Paginated thread history (v0.145.0) `[EXPERIMENTAL]`:** An experimental paginated thread history brings efficient resume, search across history, persisted thread names, sub-agent support, and memories to session management — history loads page by page instead of all at once, which matters for long-lived working directories with hundreds of sessions. Experimental: expect the surface to change between releases.[112](#fn:194)
1800:**Thread forking into sub-agents (v0.107.0):** Threads can now be forked into independent sub-agents, allowing a conversation to spawn parallel work streams that execute autonomously. This extends the existing fork model — instead of just branching the conversation, the forked thread becomes a sub-agent with its own execution context.[62](#fn:64) As of v0.117.0, sub-agents use path-based addresses (e.g., `/root/agent_a`) with structured inter-agent messaging, making multi-agent coordination more explicit and debuggable.[75](#fn:80)
1802:**Encrypted inter-agent messages (v0.138.0+):** Multi-agent v2 now **encrypts the task text a parent agent sends when it spawns or messages a sub-agent**. The message parameters of `spawn_agent`, `send_message`, and `followup_task` are marked encrypted: the Responses API encrypts the payload when the parent model emits the tool call, the locally running Codex carries only ciphertext (`InterAgentCommunication.encrypted_content`, with empty plaintext content), and the API decrypts it server-side when handing it to the receiving model.[108](#fn:190) Three practical consequences:
1804:- **Your local session logs no longer contain delegation plaintext.** You can see that a parent spawned a sub-agent and what came back, but not the exact instruction it sent. If you audit or debug multi-agent runs from session history, that record is gone; the open regression issue tracking this has no linked fix as of mid-July 2026.[109](#fn:191) Extension lifecycle events (subagent start/stop, tool execution, turn metadata — v0.133.0+) and app-server observability are the remaining hooks for reconstructing what happened.[98](#fn:175)
